# tools/ales_stereo_top_tail_witness.rish — stereo_top_tail: the top-and-tail trimmer carried into stereo, one bracket for both channels — cut only the leading and trailing joint silence, keep every interior pause on both channels in lockstep (ALES150).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES150.
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-053823_fill-ales150-lotus-stereo-top-tail.md
# Stands on: lotus/stereo_segment.rye (ALES147 — the stereo voice-activity segmenter, run once to read BOTH channels' silences over one shared frame grid, its segment_stereo, its StereoSegments, its StereoSegmentError, reused
# whole), lotus/segment.rye (ALES95 — the mono segmenter and max_segments, through ALES147), lotus/stereo_trim_silence.rye (ALES148 — the stereo silence stripper, imported by the selftest to prove the length-bracket law),
# lotus/stereo_split_silence.rye (ALES149 — the stereo auto-splitter, imported by the selftest to prove the outer-take bracket law), lotus/top_tail.rye (ALES98 — the mono top-and-tail, imported by the selftest to prove the
# lockstep law), lotus/pan.rye (ALES10 — the StereoClip, two mono Clips left/right, its balance invariant), lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2 — the Clip, its cut, its max_clip, the EditError reused whole), lotus/voiced.rye (ALES94 —
# the .silent verdict this intersects). A Lotus master is a StereoClip whose defining invariant is that left and right hold the same length; ALES147 proved the two channels segment independently, so their silences differ in
# count and boundary, and top-and-tailing each channel by its own silence would tear the stereo image apart.
#
# lotus/stereo_top_tail.rye decides ONE bracket for both channels — the silence intersection at the ends: segment both channels over one shared frame grid, mark a frame jointly-silent only where it is silent in both channels,
# keep the span from the first frame either speaker fills to the last, and cut the leading and trailing jointly-silent runs from both channels in lockstep (tail first, so the leading index stays valid). A frame silent in only
# one channel bounds the kept span; every interior pause, jointly-silent or not, is kept where it lies; only dead air at the two ends is removed. It validates the shared frame length, band, span, and floor once through ALES147's
# own up-front checks before any cut (BadThreshold on an illegal band, BadRange on a span past the samples, BadFloor on a floor above the peak ceiling, BadFrame on a zero/oversized/non-dividing length), so a refusal never trims
# one channel and leaves the other; StereoSegmentError and EditError reused whole. The crux is that it invents no new measurement and no new sample — it reads its silences through ALES147 and removes whole frames through ALES2's
# cut — so it can never drift from the segmenter it composes; what is new is the composition rule, the outer bracket of two independent silences, whose whole honesty is that running the proven mono top_tail on each channel apart
# would desync the pair.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/ales_stereo_top_tail_witness.rish

say "ales-stereo-top-tail: Language EN — stereo_top_tail trims only the leading and trailing joint silence from both channels of a stereo master, leaving every interior pause exactly where it lies. It segments both channels of the whole clip into frames of one length through ALES147's stereo segmenter, marks a frame jointly-silent only where it is silent in both channels, finds the first and last frame that is not jointly-silent, then cuts the trailing joint-silence and the leading joint-silence from both channels through ALES2's cut, tail first so the leading index stays valid. A Lotus master is two Clips heard together, one per speaker, whose defining invariant is that left and right hold the same length; a frame silent in only one channel bounds the kept span, because the other speaker still carries sound, and only a run of frames where neither speaker carries sound at the two ends is dead air. It validates the shared frame length, band, span, and floor once before any cut so a refusal never trims one channel and leaves the other."
say "ales-stereo-top-tail: Style Radiant — ALES98 top-and-tails a mono clip by cutting only its leading and trailing silence, keeping every internal pause, the single most-used silence gesture in every recorder and DAW. Carried into stereo it meets the wall the length-changing edits do: ALES147 proved the two channels segment independently, their silences differing in count and boundary, so top-and-tailing each channel by its own silence would give the two speakers different lengths and tear the stereo image apart. This rung cannot run the mono trimmer twice; it decides one bracket that serves both — the silence intersection at the ends: keep the span from the first frame either speaker fills to the last, cut only the leading and trailing jointly-silent runs, keep every interior pause. A sound panned hard to the left is real audio the right's silence must never delete. The crux is that it invents no new measurement and no new sample — it reads its silences through ALES147, which reads them through ALES95, ALES94, and the two readers those fuse, and removes whole frames through ALES2's cut, so it can never drift from the segmenter it composes. What is new is the composition rule, the outer bracket of two independent silences, and its whole honesty is that running the proven mono top_tail on each channel apart would desync the pair, where this brackets once for both and keeps them balanced. The crux stands out as four laws — the stereo span law (the result equals a by-hand keep of the original over the first-to-last not-jointly-silent frame span, per channel, across many frame lengths, floors, and splits, both channels balanced), the bracket law (it equals the original bracketed by ALES149's stereo split outer takes byte for byte, and stereo_trim_silence.len is at most stereo_top_tail.len is at most original.len), the preservation and lockstep laws (the interior jointly-silent frame kept, strictly longer than the joint trim on a gapped master, where the mono top_tail per channel desyncs to lengths twelve and four while the stereo top-tail holds both at sixteen), and the degenerate and atomicity laws (an all-jointly-silent master trimming to empty on both, a master loud on at least one channel per frame unchanged on both, a head-joint-silent master losing only its head and a tail-joint-silent only its tail, an empty clip and a ragged frame length and an inverted band and an illegal floor each refusing by name with both channels untouched and still balanced)."
say "ales-stereo-top-tail: Lens TAME — two bounded i16 Clips (left, right) trimmed at the two ends in lockstep by one shared frame length, band, floor, and split; the balance invariant is asserted on entry and exit, and all validation runs once up front through ALES147's segment_stereo (ALES92's precheck for band and span, the floor against meter's sample_peak_max, the frame-length divisibility) read-only before a single sample is cut, so a refusal leaves both channels untouched; the forward pass advances one monotonic cursor per channel across its own tiling run list, marks a frame jointly-silent only where it is silent in both, and records the first and last frame that is not jointly-silent, asserting each cursor names the run that covers the frame start; when every frame is jointly silent the whole master is cut from both channels leaving each empty, otherwise the trailing joint-silence is cut first through ALES2's cut then the leading, the in-bounds span asserted before each cut so neither can fault and leave the pair half-trimmed, and the postcondition asserts both channels hold exactly the first-to-last not-jointly-silent span and leave equal length; the stereo span law is proven against an independent by-hand reference (a second implementation reading the frame verdicts by run scan and finding first and last by hand) across eight frame lengths, four floors, and three splits, the bracket law proves the result equals ALES149's stereo split outer takes and sits between ALES148's joint trim and the original length, the lockstep law proves the mono top_tail desyncs where this does not, and the degenerate and atomicity cases leave both channels untouched and balanced; StereoSegmentError and EditError reused whole, siloed to lotus/, edits only by removing whole frames, fabricating no samples and reordering nothing, no adaptive floor, no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no device, no speaker."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/stereo_top_tail"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/stereo_top_tail.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-stereo-top-tail: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/stereo_top_tail.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-stereo-top-tail: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-stereo-top-tail: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-stereo-top-tail' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-stereo-top-tail: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-stereo-top-tail line"
